{% extends "layouts/docs" %}

{% block content %}
	<p>Precompiling turns the templates in your codebase into JavaScript modules when you build your site. They render the same HTML, with the same errors, but faster. See the <a href="/docs/benchmarks/">benchmarks</a>.</p>

	<p>Kiln never generates code while your site runs, so precompiled templates work everywhere, including Cloudflare Workers.</p>

	<h2>Precompiling at build time</h2>

	<p>Call <code>precompile</code> on an environment for each template, and save what it returns as a <code>.js</code> file. Do this in a build script or a bundler plugin.</p>

	{% apply highlight("ts") %}
		{% verbatim %}
import { mkdir, writeFile } from "node:fs/promises";
import { dirname } from "node:path";
import { createKiln } from "@lucidkiln/core";
import { fileLoader } from "@lucidkiln/core/node";
import { options } from "./src/kiln-options.ts";

const loader = fileLoader({ root: "src/templates" });
const kiln = createKiln({ ...options, loader });

for (const name of await loader.list()) {
	const file = `build/templates/${name.replace(/\.kiln$/, ".js")}`;
	await mkdir(dirname(file), { recursive: true });
	await writeFile(file, await kiln.precompile(name));
}
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<p><code>precompile</code> throws a <a href="/docs/api/errors/#error-types"><code>KilnSyntaxError</code></a> if the template has a problem, so a broken template fails the build instead of a page.</p>

	<h2>Rendering precompiled templates</h2>

	<p>Import the modules and give them to <code>memoryLoader</code> in place of the template source. Everything else stays the same.</p>

	{% apply highlight("ts") %}
		{% verbatim %}
import { createKiln, memoryLoader } from "@lucidkiln/core";
import layout from "./build/templates/layouts/base.js";
import home from "./build/templates/pages/home.js";
import { options } from "./kiln-options.ts";

const kiln = createKiln({
	...options,
	loader: memoryLoader({ "layouts/base": layout, "pages/home": home }),
});

const html = await kiln.render("pages/home", { title: "Home" });
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<p>With Vite, <code>import.meta.glob</code> can import every module at once:</p>

	{% apply highlight("ts") %}
		{% verbatim %}
import type { PrecompiledTemplate } from "@lucidkiln/core";

const modules = import.meta.glob<PrecompiledTemplate>(
	"./build/templates/**/*.js",
	{ eager: true, import: "default" },
);
const templates = Object.fromEntries(
	Object.entries(modules).map(([path, module]) => [
		path.slice("./build/templates/".length, -".js".length),
		module,
	]),
);
const kiln = createKiln({ ...options, loader: memoryLoader(templates) });
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<p>Your own loaders can return a precompiled module from <code>load</code> too, in place of the source.</p>

	<h2>Use the same options</h2>

	<p>A precompiled template is built for the options it was compiled with. Create the environment that renders it with the same <code>autoescape</code> setting, and the same functions, filters and tests. If something the module depends on is different, such as a built-in filter you replaced with your own, or the generated-code format is incompatible, rendering it fails with a <code>precompiled-mismatch</code> error. Precompile again to fix it.</p>

	<p>Build and render with the same exact version of <code>@lucidkiln/core</code>, and regenerate your modules whenever you upgrade it, including patch releases. Generated JavaScript and its runtime interface are build artifacts, not a stable public API. Kiln checks a format version and rejects known mismatches, but that check does not guarantee that modules from different package versions are compatible. Source templates and the documented public API follow semantic versioning.</p>

	<h2>Only precompile templates you trust</h2>

	<p>Precompile the templates that live in your codebase, which you review like the rest of your code. Templates written by other people, such as editors in a CMS or AI agents, should load as source. Kiln renders source without generating code, with the same <a href="/docs/api/safety/">safety rules and limits</a>.</p>

	<p>One environment can render both: a loader can return precompiled modules for your own templates and source for everything else.</p>

	<h2>What a module holds</h2>

	<p>A precompiled module holds the template's source and the JavaScript Kiln generated from it. Kiln reads the source when it loads the module, so errors point at the right line, and <code>check</code> and <code>analyze</code> work as they do for source.</p>
{% endblock %}
